description: No AI-narrative comments in any language. Comments are for non-obvious WHY only.
alwaysApply: true
---

## No AI Comments (All Languages)

Comment only where a reader would be lost without it: a constraint, an invariant, a workaround, a reason not visible in the code itself. Everywhere else, let the code speak. This applies to every language you touch.

Never write comments that restate the code, one-line docstrings on trivial functions, context-of-origin notes (`# added for the payment flow`), change history (`# was: old_value = 5`), section banners, or doc-comments that paraphrase the signature. Comments for non-obvious WHY are encouraged: hidden constraints, subtle invariants, workarounds tied to a named bug, thread-safety and lock ordering, surprising behavior a reader would misunderstand.

If a pre-edit hook blocks a Write or Edit, do not argue with it: delete the comment (most common), or rewrite it with an explicit WHY the reader could not learn from the code alone. The test: would a senior engineer reading cold learn something the code itself does not say?
